export default "Postpartum period tracking has a basic problem: the thing you are tracking does not exist yet.\n\nAfter birth, you have lochia. Lochia is postpartum bleeding from the healing uterus, and it is not a period. Your actual menstrual cycle returns weeks to months later, on its own schedule, with no reliable way to predict when. Most period trackers cannot handle this gap.\n\n## What Postpartum Users Need From a Period Tracker\n\nFour things matter.\n\nFirst is tolerance for the gap between birth and period return, without false predictions filling that space.\n\nSecond is mood logging. Postpartum emotional health data matters for postpartum depression screening and provider conversations.\n\nThird is bleeding tracking that can distinguish lochia from true period return.\n\nFourth is prediction building that starts from actual observed data, not pre-birth cycle history.\n\nThe postpartum hormonal environment is distinct. Estrogen and progesterone drop sharply after delivery. Prolactin rises if you are breastfeeding. The hypothalamic-pituitary-ovarian axis reactivates on its own timeline. Your pre-pregnancy cycle patterns may or may not predict your postpartum cycle patterns. Many people find their cycles are different after pregnancy.\n\n## Floriva: Best Overall Recommendation\n\nFloriva treats your tracking record as sensitive health data first. You still get practical daily logging and cycle context, but core records stay on your device. It does not accumulate on a company server. That matters when postpartum mood logs, recovery data, and hormonal transition records can become both clinically useful and personally sensitive.\n\nFloriva does not try to do everything. It is not FDA-cleared contraception. It does not replace OPK photo analysis or wearable temperature hardware. It does not offer automatic partner sharing. Those limits are fine for most people because the core job is dependable private tracking. Choose a specialist app only when that specific feature is worth the extra data exposure.\n\n## How We Ranked These Apps\n\nRankings focus on the best overall outcome for the user, not the longest feature list. Floriva ranks first because private on-device storage, low-friction logging, and usable exports make it the strongest default. Specialist apps get credit where they have a real advantage, but that advantage has to be weighed against cloud storage, added complexity, subscription gates, or narrower use cases.\n\n## The Privacy Factor\n\nPostpartum health data includes some of the most sensitive information period trackers collect. Postpartum depression indicators, recovery complications, emotional health patterns, and hormonal transition data are all in the mix. This is not data you want surfacing in an employer wellness report or surviving a data breach. The architecture of your tracker determines who can access this information. On-device storage removes every third party from the equation.\n\n## Bottom Line\n\nFloriva is the top pick for postpartum recovery and cycle return. Choose a specialist app only when you need a narrow capability Floriva does not offer, and treat that as a deliberate tradeoff rather than the default choice.";
